19:T73b,## The if/else-on-algorithm smell

You start with one way to do something, say, charging a card. Then PayPal shows up, so you add an `if`. Then crypto, so another `else if`. Before long one method is a tangle of branches, each branch a whole algorithm, and **every new option means re-opening and editing that method**:

```
void pay(int amt){
    if      (method == CARD)   { /* card logic   */ }
    else if (method == PAYPAL) { /* paypal logic */ }
    else if (method == CRYPTO) { /* crypto logic */ }   // grows forever
}
```

That's the smell: a single class that **knows every algorithm**, swelling with each addition and risking a regression in the others every time you touch it.

## Encapsulate each algorithm

Strategy says: pull each branch into its **own class** behind a **common interface**. `CreditCard`, `PayPal`, `Crypto` each implement `Payment.pay()`one algorithm per class, independently testable.

The **Context** (here, `Checkout`) holds a `Payment` **field** and just **delegates** to it. It no longer knows *which* algorithm runs, only that it can call `pay()`. The client picks the concrete strategy and hands it in; you can **swap it at runtime** with `setStrategy()`.

This is **composition-over-inheritance** applied to behaviour, and it makes the Context **Open/Closed**: open to new payment methods (add a class), closed to modification (Checkout never changes).

## State vs Strategy

The two have the **same shape**, a Context delegating to an interface with interchangeable implementations, but a **different intent**:

- **Strategy** is *chosen by the client* and stays stable for the operation; the objects are interchangeable algorithms.
- **State** *transitions internally*, the object swaps its own current state as events happen.

Same diagram, different story: Strategy is "pick how", State is "what am I now".c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-behavioral","activeLesson":"strategy"}],["$","$L18","strategy",{"lesson":{"id":"strategy","patternId":"lld-behavioral","title":"Strategy","kind":"pattern","difficulty":"Core","statement":"Define a family of interchangeable algorithms, put each in its own class, and let the client swap them at runtime, instead of a big if/else picking behaviour.","companies":["Amazon","Stripe","Uber"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"if/else picks the algorithm","code":["class Checkout {","    void pay(int amt){","        if      (method == CARD)   { ... }","        else if (method == PAYPAL) { ... }   // add one → edit here","    }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"One Checkout class, one pay() method, and inside it a chain of if/else, each branch a whole payment algorithm. Card here, PayPal there, crypto next.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"ctx","name":"Checkout","methods":["+ pay(amt)"],"x":1,"y":0.4,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[],"shownClassIds":["ctx"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["ctx"]},"vars":[["pay()","if/else chain"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Adding a payment method means re-opening and editing this one method, every change risks breaking the branches already there. It knows every algorithm and grows forever. Fragile, and it violates Open/Closed.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":[],"shownClassIds":["ctx"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["ctx"]},"vars":[["new method","edit pay() 😖"],["OCP","violated"]]}]},{"label":"Swap the strategy","code":["interface Payment { void pay(int amt); }","class CreditCard implements Payment { ... }   // + PayPal, Crypto","","class Checkout {","    Payment strategy;","    void setStrategy(Payment s){ strategy = s; }   // swap anytime","    void pay(int amt){ strategy.pay(amt); }        // delegate","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":4,"narration":"Flip it: Checkout HOLDS a Payment strategy, a field, and pay() just delegates to it. The hollow diamond is aggregation: Checkout has a Payment, but they stay independent objects.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"ctx","name":"Checkout","fields":["- strategy: Payment"],"methods":["+ pay(amt)","+ setStrategy(s)"],"x":0,"y":0.4},{"id":"strat","name":"Payment","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ pay(amt)"],"x":1.7,"y":0.1,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"card","name":"CreditCard","methods":["+ pay()"],"x":1,"y":1.6},{"id":"pp","name":"PayPal","methods":["+ pay()"],"x":1.95,"y":1.6},{"id":"crypto","name":"Crypto","methods":["+ pay()"],"x":2.8,"y":1.6}],"relations":[{"id":"agg","from":"ctx","to":"strat","kind":"aggregate","label":"strategy"},{"id":"c","from":"card","to":"strat","kind":"implement"},{"id":"p","from":"pp","to":"strat","kind":"implement"},{"id":"cr","from":"crypto","to":"strat","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["ctx","strat"],"shownRelIds":["agg"],"activeIds":["ctx"]},"vars":[["holds","strategy: Payment"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"CreditCard, PayPal and Crypto each implement Payment, one algorithm per class, tiny and independently testable. No giant method that knows them all.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["ctx","strat","card","pp","crypto"],"shownRelIds":["agg","c","p","cr"],"activeIds":["card","pp","crypto"]}},{"view":"sequence","codeLine":6,"narration":"At runtime the client picks PayPal, setStrategy(PayPal), then calls pay(100). Checkout simply forwards pay() to the strategy and gets ok back, never knowing which algorithm actually ran.","sequence":{"actors":[{"id":"c","label":"Client","kind":"actor"},{"id":"ck","label":"Checkout"},{"id":"s","label":"PayPal","tint":"accent"}],"messages":[{"id":"m0","from":"c","to":"ck","label":"setStrategy(PayPal)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m1","from":"c","to":"ck","label":"pay(100)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m2","from":"ck","to":"s","label":"pay(100)","kind":"call"},{"id":"m3","from":"s","to":"ck","label":"ok","kind":"return"}],"shownCount":4,"activeIndex":2}},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"Want crypto instead? setStrategy(new Crypto()), the SAME Checkout now pays with crypto. Checkout never changes; new algorithms are simply new classes. That is Open/Closed in action.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["ctx","strat","card","pp","crypto"],"shownRelIds":["agg","c","p","cr"],"activeIds":["ctx","crypto"]},"vars":[["swap algorithm","setStrategy(new Crypto())"],["at runtime ✓","Checkout unchanged"]]}]}]}}]]}]
